---
import MarketingLayout from '@/layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import SEO from '@/components/common/SEO.astro';
import { siteConfig, company } from '@/config';

const lastUpdated = siteConfig.legal.lastUpdated;
---

<SEO
  title="Editorial Independence Policy — Ownership, Review, and Disclosure"
  description="How EV Hub separates commercial ownership from editorial control: who owns the site, how content is produced and reviewed, and how conflicts of interest are disclosed."
/>

<MarketingLayout title="Editorial Independence Policy">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <div class="mb-10">
      <a href="/" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-green-600">← Back to home</a>
    </div>

    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Editorial Independence Policy</h1>
    <p class="text-gray-600 mb-10">
      Who owns EV Hub, who controls what gets published, and how we handle the fact that the two
      are not the same thing.
    </p>

    <div class="prose max-w-none space-y-8">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Why this policy exists</h2>
        <p>
          EV Hub is an information platform about importing Chinese electric vehicles. It is also
          owned by a company that works in the EV battery supply chain. Those two facts sit together
          awkwardly, and pretending otherwise would be dishonest.
        </p>
        <p>
          So we state the relationship plainly on this page: what the ownership means, what it does
          not mean, and what readers should expect from our content because of it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Who owns EV Hub</h2>
        <p>
          EV Hub is operated by <strong>{company.name}</strong> ({company.legalName}), an automotive battery manufacturer whose quality management system is certified to IATF 16949 founded in 2002 and based in Jinzhou,
          Hebei, China. {company.name} funds the site, employs or contracts the people who write for
          it, and owns the domain and the publication.
        </p>
        <p>
          That ownership is a commercial fact. It is disclosed here, on our{' '}
          <a href="/about" class="text-green-700 hover:underline">About page</a>, and in the
          organization data search engines read.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Commercial ownership ≠ editorial control</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>The owner does not decide what our editorial content says.</strong> That is the
          distinction this page exists to make concrete. In practice it means:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>
            No brand, supplier, or commercial partner gets to approve, edit, delay, or remove
            editorial content because of a business relationship or an advertising discussion.
          </li>
          <li>
            Every vehicle page, guide, and tariff figure is published on the same terms — sourced,
            dated, and open to correction — regardless of whose vehicles or products are involved.
          </li>
          <li>
            Editorial decisions (what we cover, what we recommend against, what we flag as a risk)
            are made by the analyst named on the byline, not by the commercial side of the company.
          </li>
          <li>
            If a commercial interest and an editorial finding ever conflict, the finding is
            published and the conflict is disclosed. We would rather lose a deal than publish a
            number we do not believe.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This is a policy commitment, not a claim of legal separation. {company.name} is a single
          company; there is no independent editorial board standing between the business and the
          publication. We describe the arrangement honestly rather than dressing it up.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">What we do not claim</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>
            We are <strong>not</strong> a fully independent third party with no commercial interest
            in the EV industry. Our owner builds batteries. Saying otherwise would be false.
          </li>
          <li>
            We are <strong>not</strong> a dealership, importer, customs broker, or legal adviser, and
            we do not sell vehicles. Nothing here is a quote or professional advice.
          </li>
          <li>
            We do <strong>not</strong> claim certifications, awards, testing facilities, or client
            relationships we do not have. Any credential we state is one we can point to.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Where our industrial background gives us genuine first-hand perspective, we say so
          explicitly and label it — see the{' '}
          <a href="/methodology" class="text-green-700 hover:underline">
            Evidence &amp; Data Methodology
          </a>
          . Where a claim rests on public documents, we cite the document.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">How content is produced</h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>
            <strong>Scoping.</strong> The analyst decides what a page is actually for and which
            questions it must answer before writing starts.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Research and sourcing.</strong> Facts are pinned to a named source — regulation,
            manufacturer datasheet, customs guidance, or industry report. Sources are graded by tier
            (see the <a href="/methodology" class="text-green-700 hover:underline">methodology</a>).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Drafting.</strong> Written by the named analyst. First-hand industry knowledge is
            used as context and insight, never as a substitute for a citation on a factual claim.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Data check.</strong> Every number — price, duty rate, VAT, freight assumption —
            is recomputed against its source before publication.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Review.</strong> A second pass checks for accuracy, unsupported claims, and
            conflicts of interest. The reviewer is credited on the page that carries the byline
            system.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Publish and date-stamp.</strong> Each page shows when it was last updated, so
            readers can judge whether a tariff or price still holds.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">How data is verified</h2>
        <p>
          Regulatory and cost data is traced to primary sources wherever one exists. EU
          countervailing duty rates, for example, are taken from the Commission's implementing
          regulation rather than from news summaries, and VAT rates from national tax authorities.
          Where only secondary sources exist, we say which kind and treat the figure as an estimate.
        </p>
        <p>
          The full method — including the tiering system and the formula behind landed-cost figures —
          is published in the <a href="/landed-cost-methodology" class="text-green-700 hover:underline">
            Landed Cost Methodology
          </a>{' '}
          and the <a href="/methodology" class="text-green-700 hover:underline">Evidence &amp; Data
          Methodology</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">How authors are credited</h2>
        <p>
          Every substantive page carries an attribution block naming who wrote it, who reviewed it,
          how the data was verified, and when it was last updated. We do not publish anonymous
          analysis, and we do not put a byline on a page no named person stands behind. The roles
          and the byline system are set out on the{' '}
          <a href="/authors" class="text-green-700 hover:underline">Authors &amp; Review Standards</a>
          {' '}page.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Conflict-of-interest disclosure</h2>
        <p>We disclose a relationship when it could reasonably affect how a reader weighs a page. That includes:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Any commercial relationship with a brand, supplier, or service provider mentioned in the content.</li>
          <li>Any first-hand business experience that informs a judgement, labelled as such.</li>
          <li>Any material interest the author holds in a company or product under discussion.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If you believe a page needs a disclosure that is missing, tell us — see contact details
          below.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Advertising, sponsorship, and affiliate links</h2>
        <p>
          We do not sell vehicles and we do not take payment for a favourable review. If we ever
          introduce sponsored content or affiliate links, they will be labelled as such on the page
          itself, and sponsorship will not buy editorial conclusions.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          When we get something wrong, we fix it and note the change. Material corrections to a
          dated figure or a factual claim are recorded on the page, not quietly swapped out. To
          report an error, email{' '}
          <a href={`mailto:${company.email}`} class="text-green-700 hover:underline">{company.email}</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="border-t border-gray-200 pt-8 text-sm text-gray-600">
        <p>
          Published by <strong>{company.name}</strong> · {company.legalName}
          <br/>Editorial contact: <strong>{company.author}</strong>, {company.authorRole}
          <br/>Last updated: {lastUpdated}
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</MarketingLayout>
